In the Chancery Division, case management conferences (CMC/CCMC) are handled as follows:

- **When the CMC is fixed:** The court fixes a date for a **CMC/CCMC after the Directions Questionnaires (DQs) have been filed**. It normally has a **time estimate of 1½ hours plus 1 hour pre-reading** (unless the parties agree a different estimate for both). [6]

- **Remote attendance:** A CMC/CCMC **with a time estimate of half a day or less** (and **no more than 90 minutes pre-reading**) will **take place remotely unless the court orders otherwise**. [6]

- **Who attends:** Where possible, the advocate instructed (or expected to be instructed) to appear at trial should attend. If there is more than one advocate, **there’s no requirement that all attend**; the most senior may not always be needed, and the court may rely on junior advocates for many case management issues. [7]

- **Preparation / pre-conditions (DQs):**
  - **Form N149C** requires parties to file and serve DQs and attempt to agree directions **by the deadline specified**. The deadline may be extended by agreement for up to **28 days**, but a **consent order** recording the extension must be agreed/filed and **approved by the court**. [8]
  - Failure to file the documents required by Form N149C by the deadline (original or revised) may lead to **strike out or other sanctions**. [9]

- **What is discussed at the CMC:** The most important matters include:
  - the **issues** and how best to try them, including **where/by whom**;
  - **ADR** proposals;
  - the **DRD/LCCDRD** and permission for **expert evidence**;
  - reviewing/considering the **case summary**, **list of issues for trial**, **list of issues for the CMC/CCMC**, and **notes/skeleton arguments**;
  - discussing requirements with parties/advocates;
  - reviewing/approving/amending/rejecting proposals for **disclosure**;
  - considering whether the trial judge is assisted by a **core bundle** or **narrative chronology**;
  - **fixing the pre-trial timetable** as fully as possible;
  - (if appropriate) making a **costs management order** (or directing a separate costs management hearing);
  - (if appropriate) staying proceedings to permit **ADR**; and
  - (if appropriate) use of **IT** to assist case management (including at trial). [4]

- **Expert evidence at the CMC:** Usually, the necessity/scope/timing and sequence of expert reports will be considered at a CMC. [3]

- **CMC on the documents (exceptional):**
  - In **exceptional cases**, the CMC/CCMC may be on the documents if issues are straightforward and an oral hearing can’t be justified. [5]
  - To request this, parties must file the **case management bundle** with an **agreed draft order at least 1 week before** the CMC/CCMC date. [5]
  - It’s unlikely a case involving **extended disclosure (other than on Models A or B)**, **expert evidence**, or **preliminary issues** will be suitable for a CMC/CCMC on the documents. [5]

- **Which court track manages the case (context for CM/CMC):** In ChD B&PCs London, cases are managed on one of four tracks (e.g., case management by **Master and trial by HCJ/Section 9 Judge/Deputy HCJ** as the most common track, or different combinations involving docketed HCJs/Masters). [10]

**Search depth tip:** If you need the *process for the first CMC/CCMC specifically* (e.g., contents of the case management bundle), you may want to run a **Thorough** search.
